1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,552
Jane:

2
00:00:01,552 --> 00:00:03,750
Hello, Jane Fairbanks speaking.

3
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:04,472
Frank:

4
00:00:04,472 --> 00:00:05,094
Oh, good morning.

5
00:00:05,094 --> 00:00:07,064
My name’s Frank Pritchard.

6
00:00:07,064 --> 00:00:10,010
I’ve just retired and moved to Southoe.

7
00:00:10,010 --> 00:00:12,588
I’d like to become a volunteer,

8
00:00:12,588 --> 00:00:16,270
and I gather you co-ordinate voluntary work in the village.

9
00:00:16,270 --> 00:00:16,515
Jane:

10
00:00:16,515 --> 00:00:17,250
That’s right.

11
00:00:17,250 --> 00:00:17,575
Frank:

12
00:00:17,575 --> 00:00:19,850
What sort of thing could I do?

13
00:00:19,850 --> 00:00:20,242
Jane:

14
00:00:20,242 --> 00:00:23,382
Well, we need help with the village library.

15
00:00:23,382 --> 00:00:26,129
We borrow books from the town library,

16
00:00:26,129 --> 00:00:28,091
and individuals also donate them.

17
00:00:28,091 --> 00:00:33,193
So, one thing you could do is get involved in collecting them – if

18
00:00:33,193 --> 00:00:35,155
you’ve got a car,

19
00:00:35,155 --> 00:00:35,940
that is.

20
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:36,300
Frank:

21
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:36,660
Yes,

22
00:00:36,660 --> 00:00:38,100
that’s no problem.

23
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:38,431
Jane:

24
00:00:38,431 --> 00:00:42,730
The times are pretty flexible so we can arrange it to suit you.

25
00:00:42,730 --> 00:00:47,360
Another thing is the records that we keep of the books we’re given,

26
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:51,329
and those we borrow and need to return to the town library.

27
00:00:51,329 --> 00:00:55,959
It would be very useful to have another person to help keep them up

28
00:00:55,959 --> 00:00:56,620
to date.

29
00:00:56,620 --> 00:00:57,011
Frank:

30
00:00:57,011 --> 00:00:57,403
Right.

31
00:00:57,403 --> 00:01:02,490
I’m used to working on a computer – I presume they’re computerised?

32
00:01:02,490 --> 00:01:02,930
Jane:

33
00:01:02,930 --> 00:01:03,810
Oh yes.

34
00:01:03,810 --> 00:01:04,131
Frank:

35
00:01:04,131 --> 00:01:05,413
Is the library purpose-built?

36
00:01:05,413 --> 00:01:09,260
I haven’t noticed it when I’ve walked round the village.

37
00:01:09,260 --> 00:01:09,566
Jane:

38
00:01:09,566 --> 00:01:13,847
No, we simply have the use of a room in the village hall, the

39
00:01:13,847 --> 00:01:14,458
West Room.

40
00:01:14,458 --> 00:01:17,210
It’s on the left as you go in.

41
00:01:17,210 --> 00:01:17,510
Frank:

42
00:01:17,510 --> 00:01:20,510
I must go and have a look inside the hall.

43
00:01:20,510 --> 00:01:20,820
Jane:

44
00:01:20,820 --> 00:01:22,680
Yes, it’s a nice building.

45
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:22,996
Frank:

46
00:01:22,996 --> 00:01:26,788
Do you run a lunch club in the village for elderly people?

47
00:01:26,788 --> 00:01:29,000
I know a lot of places do.

48
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:29,351
Jane:

49
00:01:29,351 --> 00:01:31,810
Yes, we have a very successful club.

50
00:01:31,810 --> 00:01:32,148
Frank:

51
00:01:32,148 --> 00:01:35,860
I could help with transport, if that’s of any use.

52
00:01:35,860 --> 00:01:36,248
Jane:

53
00:01:36,248 --> 00:01:37,024
Ooo definitely.

54
00:01:37,024 --> 00:01:40,128
People come to the club from neighbouring villages,

55
00:01:40,128 --> 00:01:43,620
and we’re always in need of more drivers.

56
00:01:43,620 --> 00:01:43,998
Frank:

57
00:01:43,998 --> 00:01:48,906
And does the club have groups that focus on a particular hobby, too?

58
00:01:48,906 --> 00:01:54,192
I could get involved in one or two, particularly if there are any art

59
00:01:54,192 --> 00:01:54,570
groups.

60
00:01:54,570 --> 00:01:54,925
Jane:

61
00:01:54,925 --> 00:01:55,279
Excellent.

62
00:01:55,279 --> 00:01:59,890
I’ll find out where we need help and get back to you.

63
00:01:59,890 --> 00:02:00,354
Frank:

64
00:02:00,354 --> 00:02:00,817
Fine.

65
00:02:00,817 --> 00:02:03,599
What about help for individual residents.

66
00:02:03,599 --> 00:02:06,380
Do you arrange that at all?

67
00:02:06,380 --> 00:02:06,774
Jane:

68
00:02:06,774 --> 00:02:09,529
Yes, we do it as a one-off.

69
00:02:09,529 --> 00:02:11,498
In fact, there’s Mrs.

70
00:02:11,498 --> 00:02:11,891
Carroll.

71
00:02:11,891 --> 00:02:15,434
She needs a lift to the hospital next week,

72
00:02:15,434 --> 00:02:18,190
and we’re struggling to find someone.

73
00:02:18,190 --> 00:02:18,524
Frank:

74
00:02:18,524 --> 00:02:19,860
When’s her appointment?

75
00:02:19,860 --> 00:02:20,284
Jane:

76
00:02:20,284 --> 00:02:21,133
On Tuesday.

77
00:02:21,133 --> 00:02:23,680
It would take the whole morning.

78
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:23,946
Frank:

79
00:02:23,946 --> 00:02:25,010
I could do that.

80
00:02:25,010 --> 00:02:25,360
Jane:

81
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:25,711
Oh,

82
00:02:25,711 --> 00:02:27,113
that would be great.

83
00:02:27,113 --> 00:02:27,814
Thank you.

84
00:02:27,814 --> 00:02:32,721
And also, next week, we’re arranging to have some work done to Mr.

85
00:02:32,721 --> 00:02:37,978
Selsbury’s house before he moves, as he isn’t healthy enough to do it

86
00:02:37,978 --> 00:02:38,329
himself.

87
00:02:38,329 --> 00:02:41,483
We’ve got some people to decorate his kitchen,

88
00:02:41,483 --> 00:02:44,988
but if you could do some weeding in his garden,

89
00:02:44,988 --> 00:02:46,390
that would be wonderful.

90
00:02:46,390 --> 00:02:46,829
Frank:

91
00:02:46,829 --> 00:02:47,267
OK.

92
00:02:47,267 --> 00:02:49,021
I’d enjoy that.

93
00:02:49,021 --> 00:02:52,530
And presumably the day and time are flexible.

94
00:02:52,530 --> 00:02:52,854
Jane:

95
00:02:52,854 --> 00:02:53,501
Oh yes.

96
00:02:53,501 --> 00:02:55,765
Just say when would suit you best,

97
00:02:55,765 --> 00:02:57,383
and we’ll let Mr.

98
00:02:57,383 --> 00:02:58,030
Selsbury know.

99
00:02:58,030 --> 00:02:58,760
Frank:

100
00:02:58,760 --> 00:02:59,490
Good

101
00:02:59,490 --> 00:02:59,881
Jane:

102
00:02:59,881 --> 00:03:03,009
The volunteers group also organises monthly social events,

103
00:03:03,009 --> 00:03:07,310
which is a great way to meet other people, of course.

104
00:03:07,310 --> 00:03:07,730
Frank:

105
00:03:07,730 --> 00:03:08,150
Uhuh.

106
00:03:08,150 --> 00:03:08,557
Jane:

107
00:03:08,557 --> 00:03:14,256
So next month, on the 19th of October, we’re holding a quiz – a

108
00:03:14,256 --> 00:03:17,920
couple of residents are great at planning unusual ones,

109
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:20,770
and we always fill the village hall.

110
00:03:20,770 --> 00:03:21,138
Frank:

111
00:03:21,138 --> 00:03:22,611
That sounds like fun.

112
00:03:22,611 --> 00:03:24,820
Can I do anything to help?

113
00:03:24,820 --> 00:03:25,220
Jane:

114
00:03:25,220 --> 00:03:25,619
Well,

115
00:03:25,619 --> 00:03:31,214
because of the number of people, we need plenty of refreshments for halfway through.

116
00:03:31,214 --> 00:03:35,210
So, if you could provide any, we’d be grateful.

117
00:03:35,210 --> 00:03:35,480
Frank:

118
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:36,830
I’m sure I could.

119
00:03:36,830 --> 00:03:38,720
I’ll think about what to make,

120
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:39,800
and let you know.

121
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:40,204
Jane:

122
00:03:40,204 --> 00:03:41,012
Thank you.

123
00:03:41,012 --> 00:03:46,670
Then on November the 18th, we’re holding a dance, also in the village

124
00:03:46,670 --> 00:03:47,074
hall.

125
00:03:47,074 --> 00:03:53,136
We’ve booked a band that specialises in music of the 1930s – they’ve been

126
00:03:53,136 --> 00:03:53,540
before,

127
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:58,390
and we’ve had a lot of requests to bring them back.

128
00:03:58,390 --> 00:03:58,680
Frank:

129
00:03:58,680 --> 00:04:00,420
I’m not really a dancer,

130
00:04:00,420 --> 00:04:03,030
but I’d like to do something to help.

131
00:04:03,030 --> 00:04:03,401
Jane:

132
00:04:03,401 --> 00:04:05,630
Well, we sell tickets in advance,

133
00:04:05,630 --> 00:04:10,830
and having an extra person to check them at the door, as people arrive,

134
00:04:10,830 --> 00:04:16,030
would be good – it can be quite a bottleneck if everyone arrives at once!

135
00:04:16,030 --> 00:04:16,369
Frank:

136
00:04:16,369 --> 00:04:18,400
OK, I’m happy with that.

137
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:18,747
Jane:

138
00:04:18,747 --> 00:04:22,215
We’re also arranging a New Year’s Eve party.

139
00:04:22,215 --> 00:04:25,684
We’re expecting that to be a really big event,

140
00:04:25,684 --> 00:04:30,540
so instead of the village hall, it’ll be held in the Mountfort Hotel.

141
00:04:30,540 --> 00:04:31,080
Frank:

142
00:04:31,080 --> 00:04:31,620
The …?

143
00:04:31,620 --> 00:04:32,129
Jane:

144
00:04:32,129 --> 00:04:32,638
Mountfort.

145
00:04:32,638 --> 00:04:33,656
M-O-U-N-T-F-O-R-T Hotel.

146
00:04:33,656 --> 00:04:36,711
It isn’t in Southoe itself,

147
00:04:36,711 --> 00:04:41,292
but it’s only a couple of miles away.

148
00:04:41,292 --> 00:04:47,401
The hotel will be providing dinner and we’ve booked a band.

149
00:04:47,401 --> 00:04:53,001
The one thing we haven’t got yet is a poster.

150
00:04:53,001 --> 00:04:59,110
That isn’t something you could do, by any chance, is it?

151
00:04:59,110 --> 00:04:59,496
Frank:

152
00:04:59,496 --> 00:05:00,655
Well actually, yes.

153
00:05:00,655 --> 00:05:03,746
Before I retired I was a graphic designer,

154
00:05:03,746 --> 00:05:06,450
so that’s right up my street.

155
00:05:06,450 --> 00:05:06,797
Jane:

156
00:05:06,797 --> 00:05:07,492
Oh perfect!

157
00:05:07,492 --> 00:05:09,575
I’ll give you the details,

158
00:05:09,575 --> 00:05:12,700
and then perhaps you could send me a draft …

159
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:13,133
Frank:

160
00:05:13,133 --> 00:05:14,000
Of course.
